<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<html>
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8"/>
  </head>
  <body>
    <ul id="3c7V5ztI">
      <li id="pVf">
        <p>Early History of Psycholinguistics</p>
      </li>
      <li id="Cqo">
        <p><span>(Notes from Levelt’s </span><em>A History of Psycholinguistics</em><span>)</span></p>
      </li>
      <li id="f4">
        <p/>
      </li>
      <li id="fs">
        <p>Wilhelm von Humboldt (1767-1835)</p>
        <ul>
          <li id="DAN">
            <p>Philosophy of State</p>
          </li>
          <li id="Dev">
            <p>Philosophy of Education</p>
          </li>
          <li id="UZs">
            <p>Diplomat</p>
          </li>
          <li id="R26">
            <p>Linguist</p>
            <ul>
              <li id="L72">
                <p>Studied Basque</p>
              </li>
              <li id="0OC">
                <p>When he died, he was working on a book about the Kawi language in Java</p>
              </li>
              <li id="MnK">
                <p>View of language</p>
                <ul>
                  <li id="MtL">
                    <p>Language is an activity - language is something we do</p>
                  </li>
                  <li id="Bik">
                    <p>This is very different than early views that saw language as e.g. something that was written down, or as a spoken product, like an epic poem</p>
                  </li>
                  <li id="b0p">
                    <p>Humboldt also mentions the existence of “inner speech”, but writes very little about it</p>
                  </li>
                  <li id="YzK">
                    <p>Has early ideas about linguistic relativity, but not in the strong Whorfian sense - he talks more about certain language having certain affordances, but says that any idea can be expressed in any language</p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li id="UiU">
        <p>Johann Friedrich Herbert (1776-1841)</p>
        <ul>
          <li id="dL1">
            <p>Perhaps the first psychologist?</p>
          </li>
          <li id="z7F">
            <p>The concept of an “idea”</p>
            <ul>
              <li id="jS9">
                <p>Psychology is our inner life</p>
              </li>
              <li id="585">
                <p>Part of that inner life is conscious, the other part is our unconscious</p>
              </li>
              <li id="Fm-">
                <p>Ideas inter our consciousness through our senses or through memories</p>
              </li>
              <li id="98u">
                <p>Ideas are always in motion, and interact with each other like billiard balls (mental mechanics)</p>
              </li>
              <li id="o6c">
                <p>Consciousness is like a stage where ideas appear, interact, and then disappear offstage</p>
              </li>
              <li id="PkQ">
                <p>When ideas become associated with each other, Herbert called this “apperception” (we will return to this with Wundt)</p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li id="HKW">
        <p>Heymann Steinthal (1823-1855)</p>
        <ul>
          <li id="KEu">
            <p>Inspired by Herbert</p>
            <ul>
              <li id="vS8">
                <p>Only one idea can be “onstage” at a time</p>
                <ul>
                  <li id="MHv">
                    <p>Through interaction, ideas can gain “energy” and it is this energy that pushes them onto the stage of consciousness</p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li id="Jsf">
                <p>Ideas can interact in different ways</p>
                <ul>
                  <li id="bie">
                    <p>Fusion: I see my cat, then later I see him again. The first image I have fuses with the later images I have, and they become one: my abstract idea of my cat</p>
                  </li>
                  <li id="ekP">
                    <p>Entanglement: Ideas can become connected to each other, but still separate. My idea of “my cat” can become entangled with my idea of “animal”, and also “pet”</p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li id="7t_">
        <p>Max Müller (1823-1900)</p>
        <ul>
          <li id="KR4">
            <p>Studied Sanskrit</p>
          </li>
          <li id="5h2">
            <p>He took the 1100 Sanskrit roots that Panini had written down two thousand years earlier and reduced them to 121 core concepts. “These 121 concepts constitute the stock-in-trade with which I maintain that every thought that has passed through the mind of India, so far as it is known to us in its literature, has been expressed.” (Levelt pg. 32)</p>
          </li>
          <li id="FJP">
            <p>“Language is the true autobiography of mind.” (quoted in Levelt, pg. 32)</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li id="ld">
        <p>Wilhelm Wundt (1832-1920)</p>
        <ul>
          <li id="lr">
            <p>Medical doctor - physiology</p>
          </li>
          <li id="Tu">
            <p>“Contributions to the Theory of Sense Perception” (1858–1862)</p>
          </li>
          <li id="Ts">
            <p>“Lectures on Human and Animal Psychology” (1863–1864)</p>
          </li>
          <li id="bW">
            <p>“Principles of Physiological Psychology” (1874)</p>
          </li>
          <li id="fH">
            <p>Laboratory of experimental psychology</p>
          </li>
          <li id="jQ">
            <p>Timing - clocks, electronic </p>
          </li>
          <li id="CD">
            <p>Visitors to the lab included</p>
            <ul>
              <li id="LgM">
                <p>Edward Sapir</p>
              </li>
              <li id="WS">
                <p>Benjamin Lee Whorf</p>
              </li>
              <li id="AE">
                <p>Edmund Husserel (phenomenology)</p>
              </li>
              <li id="sh">
                <p>Franz Boas (anthropology)</p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li id="rQe">
            <p>Wundt’s “apperception” </p>
            <ul>
              <li id="yFn">
                <p>Redefinition of Herbert’s word</p>
              </li>
              <li id="PQz">
                <p><span>“The state, characterised by a specific sensation, which accompanies the clearer focusing of some mental content we call </span><em>attention</em><span>; the specific process by which some mental content is brought into clear focus [we call] apperception”.</span></p>
              </li>
              <li id="erE">
                <p>Levelt: Wundt’s “apperception” is very similar to the modern idea of “executive control”</p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li id="QQ">
            <p>Volition</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li id="8g">
        <p>Donders (1818-1889)</p>
        <ul>
          <li id="HP">
            <p>Hermann von Helmholtz () had shown neural transmission takes time</p>
          </li>
          <li id="_kS">
            <p>Donders showed that cognitive function also takes time</p>
            <ul>
              <li id="rt9">
                <p>Simple reaction time is faster than choice reaction time</p>
              </li>
              <li id="CR8">
                <p>Invented the go/no-go task (with stimulation of feet!)</p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li id="vx8">
        <p>James McKeen Cattell (1860-1944)</p>
        <ul>
          <li id="0gG">
            <p>Wundt’s PhD student</p>
          </li>
          <li id="afY">
            <p>Time it takes to read letter</p>
          </li>
          <li id="kpK">
            <p>Word superiority effect</p>
            <ul>
              <li id="APV">
                <p>RED is faster than DER</p>
              </li>
              <li id="j0i">
                <p>BRANE is faster than BENAR</p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li id="kt4">
            <p/>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li id="3C">
        <p>1951</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </body>
</html>
